  "verdict": "YES - a bulk single-pass architecture reproduces the proven geometry, and the per-parcel cost is dominated by orchestration and verification, not by geometry. Measured: 141.98 ms/parcel end-to-end (124.55 ms/parcel compute-only) over the real 5,792-parcel zoned Bastrop cohort, versus the 522 ms/parcel baseline - a 3.7x end-to-end / 4.2x compute-only speedup - with ZERO live HTTP calls in the loop (tripwire-enforced) and 5 DB queries plus 9 bulk HTTP pages for the ENTIRE cohort, replacing ~6-7 sequential round trips per parcel. The decisive number is the offset core itself: 1,008 ms for 3,548 envelopes = 0.28 ms per envelope. Envelope geometry is ~0.2% of the current 522 ms/parcel budget. Everything else is I/O the bulk pass removes, plus verification instruments (mechanical verify + ground truth = 81% of the remaining loop) that are QA, not construction. Parity is proven where it was measured: operator twelve 12/12 exact (max 0.041 ft), block13 5/7 with both residuals carrying correct setback magnitudes and diverging only in edge-role ORDER, and 223/250 on a random promoted sample. The residual is root-caused to one omitted pure-function step (R30 relabelBoundaryEdgesFromRoadLabels), not to the geometry math, which is the identical shared code. A production bulk pass that adds that one call should be expected to close the gap at no I/O cost. Caveat stated plainly: this prototype computed and measured only - it never wrote, so it does not exercise write-then-verify or the promote gate, which carry their own per-parcel cost.",
